focus id

prism @(id) {
  focus prism
  project prism
  split prism
  zoom prism
  refract prism
}

prism @prism {
  focus focus
  project project
  split split
  zoom zoom
  refract refract
}

abstract io tick(type) -> tock(type) { \ }

project in(prism)
project out(prism)

# Parametric type forms — the five operations applied at the type layer.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-parametric-types-and-fp-heritage.md:
# the same five verbs that act on values also act on types, with the
# same names one universe up. Two are declared here as first-class
# parametric forms; the other three (focus(T), project(T), split(T))
# fall out of the existing kernel forms (focus is type(id); project is
# field selection; split is the | disjunction in `type x = a | b`).
#
# zoom(T): annotation-only parametric. Same bytes, different declared
# shape. Functor-shaped: zoom satisfies identity (zoom id = id) and
# composition (zoom (f . g) = zoom f . zoom g). The runtime cost is
# zero by construction — the type checker distinguishes T(U1) from
# T(U2) via content address; the bytes don't change.
#
# refract(T): verified-construction parametric. Building a refract(T)
# value IS the proof that the construction settled. Monad-shaped:
# refract satisfies left identity, right identity, and associativity.
# Content-addressing gives associativity for free at composition time
# (oid concatenation is associative; the substrate enforces it).
#
# Both are type-layer applications of the existing five Prism
# operations. No new primitives — the algebra is closed at five.
# The @epistemologic/property/laws/{functor,monad} grammars
# verify the laws structurally.
zoom zoom(type) -> type { \ }
refract refract(type) -> type { \ }

out in
out id
out @
out @prism
out zoom
out refract
